{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "**A Whirlwind in Japan: Cherry Blossoms and Sushi Nights**\n\nJapan stole my heart in just seven days. Arriving in Tokyo, neon lights and futuristic trains set the pace—easily lost amid bullet trains and bustling streets. But it was Kyoto that charmed me: temples draped in lantern light, bamboo forests whispering secrets, and the unforgettable sight of cherry blossoms (*sakura*) dancing in the breeze. I joined locals under the trees at Maruyama Park, picnicking on *bento* boxes while the petals drifted like snow.\n\nFood became my guide. In Tokyo’s tiny *izakayas*, I sipped sake and shared plates of *yakitori* with strangers. The Michelin-starred sushi at Sukiyabashi Jiro was a revelation—each bite a masterpiece of texture and flavor. But the best meals were spontaneous: *takoyaki* from a street cart in Osaka, ramen in Hiroshima so rich it could stand as a monument.\n\nOff the beaten path, I hiked the trails of Takachiho Gorge, where legend says the sun and moon once raced. The misty cliffs and waterfalls made me feel tiny—and profoundly peaceful. Then there was Nara, where a friendly deer nuzzled my palm for a cracker, and the towering Buddha at Todai-ji made me laugh at how small I’d grown.\n\nSleeping on a futon in a ryokan (traditional inn) in Hakone, surrounded by steaming baths and mountain views, felt like waking up in a dream. As my flight home took off, I already knew Japan wouldn’t let go so easily. Maybe next time, I’ll linger longer—or come back just to chase another season of *sakura*.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.07225250452756882}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -0.42479637265205383}, {"token": " Wh", "logprob": -0.0445331335067749}, {"token": "irl", "logprob": -0.01628841459751129}, {"token": "wind", "logprob": -7.986703712958843e-05}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.1293786764144897}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob"